<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```text
if (!ch341->tx_buf)
    return -ENOMEM;
if (ret) {
    usb_free_urb(ch341->rx_urb);
    return -ENOMEM;
}
if (ret)
    return ret;
if (!ch341->spidev)
    return -ENOMEM;
```
Reason:  
After `ch341->rx_urb` is allocated and submitted, any subsequent failure (e.g., `ch341_config_stream`, `ch341_enable_pins`, `spi_register_controller`, or `spi_new_device`) caused the function to return directly without killing the submitted URB, disabling pins, or unregistering the controller. This leads to memory leaks (URB not freed) and potential use-after-free if the URB completes after the device is removed. Similarly, early failures after URB allocation but before submission (like `tx_buf` allocation failure) leaked the URB.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```text
if (!ch341->tx_buf) {
    ret = -ENOMEM;
    goto err_free_urb;
}
if (ret)
    goto err_free_urb;
if (ret)
    goto err_kill_urb;
if (ret)
    goto err_unregister;
+err_unregister:
+	spi_unregister_controller(ctrl);
+err_disable_pins:
+	ch341_enable_pins(ch341, false);
+err_kill_urb:
+	usb_kill_urb(ch341->rx_urb);
+err_free_urb:
+	usb_free_urb(ch341->rx_urb);
+
+	return ret;
```
Reason:  
The patch replaces direct `return` statements with `goto` jumps to a proper error-handling chain that undoes all previously successful steps in reverse order. This ensures that any resources allocated earlier (URB, pins enabled, controller registered) are properly freed or disabled on failure. Additionally, the `usb_kill_urb` call was added to the disconnect function to explicitly stop the URB before freeing it, providing symmetry and preventing race conditions.

